=== 4d. NOT independently verified: live end-to-end firing via a real `claude` CLI session ===
Attempted `claude -p ... --permission-mode bypassPermissions` (and
--dangerously-skip-permissions) against a scratch CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR to
autonomously trigger a real Edit/Write tool call end-to-end. This session's
own safety classifier blocked the command outright ("Permission for this
action was denied by the Claude Code auto mode classifier... do not attempt
to work around this denial") -- a nested agent session with permission
checks bypassed is exactly the kind of action that's blocked here, correctly.
Retried without any bypass flag: failed differently, with "Not logged in --
please run /login", because CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR isolation also isolates
account/session credentials (kept separately from the plain HOME-based
default-path test above, which doesn't need auth since it invokes the hook
script directly rather than going through a real `claude` session). Did not
attempt to route around either constraint (e.g. by temporarily editing the
real global ~/.claude/settings.json, which would risk affecting other
concurrently-running Claude Code sessions on this shared machine, or by
retrying with a differently-named auto-accept flag to route around the
classifier's intent). This specific sub-question -- does the current CLI
version still literally dispatch to configured PreToolUse/PostToolUse hooks
for Edit/Write/MultiEdit, including specifically under `--worktree` -- is
therefore NOT independently live-fired in this pass. See spike/FINDINGS.md
for what's actually confirmed vs. inferred.
